  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Mild daytime temperatures through today.\n\n-  Significant weather change coming tonight into Friday.\n\nToday will offer the last day this week of mild day-time \ntemperatures, with highs rising to around 10 degrees above normal.\nStable conditions this morning led to valley fog development, \nwith fog expected to burn off for most areas by 800-900 AM MDT. \nWater vapor imagery this morning shows a closed low spinning over\nCalifornia. This low will begin to track northeastward into the \nGreat Basin by later this afternoon. High-resolution models \nsuggest moisture and instability will quickly move south to north,\nwith showers and isolated thunderstorms developing across central\nIdaho and spreading into Lemhi and Idaho Counties in Idaho. This \nactivity will track into southwest Montana by early evening, with\nthe threat for thunderstorms decreasing as the sun begins to set.\n\nA significant change arrives tonight into Friday, as a low \npressure system and cold front tracks across the region. This\nsystem will bring colder temperatures and widespread precipitation.\nPrecipitation amounts have continued to trend higher over the \nlast 24 hours, as model guidance tracks a mid-level low along the \ndivide in southwest Montana by Friday morning. Widespread \nprecipitation will wrap around the west-northwest area of this low\nwith prolonged rainfall for much of Friday along and south of the\nInterstate 90 corridor. Residents in north-central Idaho and \nnorthwest Montana will see the majority of rain after midnight \ninto mid-morning Friday as the cold front passes. The cold air \nwill lag behind most of the precipitation, with minor snow amounts\nfocused above 6,000 feet. \n\nThe low will depart to the east Friday night, with dry conditions\nreturning across the region on Saturday. Forecast uncertainty\nbegins to increase by Sunday. Model ensemble cluster analysis \ncontinues to display a wide array of outcomes as a closed low \ndrops southward from the Gulf of Alaska. Roughly 40 percent of \nensemble members push the closed low across the Northern Rockies \nby late Sunday or Monday, with the remaining members suggesting \nthe low may stall or retrograde to the west. Overall, the forecast\nleans on the NBM blend, which suggests mostly dry conditions \nSunday into Monday, with shower chances returning by Tuesday and \nWednesday when the majority of ensemble members push this low \nfurther east towards the Northern Rockies.",
